{
  "abstract": "Let a biplanar graph contain the join of a six-clique and a five-cycle. We prove that every prescribed nine-coloring of this core extends if, in each exterior component, at most three vertices have fewer than three neighbors on the cycle. All regular vertices, those with at least three such neighbors, can use the six prescribed clique colors. The three exceptions may be pairwise adjacent. Two explicit thirteen-vertex graphs show that three rim neighbors are necessary for the uniform restriction to clique colors. Each has two adjacent exterior vertices with the same five hub neighbors and admits a partition into two triangulations. Fixing the regular vertices, we also prove that the exceptions with at least one rim neighbor that individually prevent a six-coloring together with the clique and the regular vertices induce a forest. This gives an extension theorem with arbitrarily many exceptional vertices and an odd cycle with two available rim colors. The proof compares cuts of overlapping critical subgraphs against the edge capacity of the two planar layers. Disjoint critical seven-cliques give separating sets that force a deficit in both layers. For vertices with four rim neighbors, we prove a sharp three-position bound on the missed rim vertices and a separate extension theorem allowing four arbitrary exceptions.",
